window.FATTOS_DOCS_BUNDLE = {
  "architecture.md": "# Architecture notes\n\n## Execution model\nThe scheduler is fixed-capacity and cooperative. Each callback is a periodic\njob. When several jobs are due, the highest numeric priority is selected\nfirst; equal priorities rotate through the scan so no task is\nsystematically executed last every cycle. After one callback returns, the\ncaller invokes `fattos_run_once()` again, or drains a bounded budget with\n`fattos_run_pending()` — the bound guarantees a drain loop can never spin\nforever and freeze its caller.\n\nTimers do not run on their own context: `fattos_timer_service()` executes\nexpired timer callbacks in the calling task's context. Register it once via\n`fattos_timers_register()` and keep timer callbacks as short as task\ncallbacks.\n\n## Timing assumptions\nThe public API expresses durations in milliseconds and converts them to\nticks with `fattos_ms_to_ticks()` (rounds up, never yields 0 for a non-zero\nduration). Any `FATTOS_TICK_HZ` works: at 1 kHz, 1 ms is exactly one tick;\nat slower rates all due checks and timeouts simply resolve at tick\ngranularity. The due test uses signed modular subtraction and is safe for\nintervals less than 2^31 ticks. Keep task periods far below that limit.\n\n## Interrupt rules\n- `fattos_tick_isr()` must only be called by the configured periodic timer ISR.\n- Do not invoke task callbacks from interrupts.\n- Do not call blocking drivers from high-priority callbacks.\n- On platforms where 32-bit reads are not atomic, protect tick reads.\n- Register tasks before enabling interrupts that dispatch application code.\n- The irq lock (`fattos_platform_irq_lock/unlock`) is what makes queues,\n  the pool, timer/watchdog registries and safety heartbeats safe across the\n  ISR/task boundary; ports must save and restore real interrupt state.\n\n## Instrumentation\nEvery dispatch measures its callback at tick resolution:\n`exec_last_ticks`, `exec_max_ticks`, `overrun_count` (ran longer than its\nperiod), `missed_releases` (releases skipped because the task was late)\nand `jitter_max_ticks` (worst lateness between due time and dispatch —\nthe scheduling-latency WCET to validate on target).\nAn overrun additionally invokes the registered fault hook\n(`fattos_set_fault_hook`) in task context — use it to latch an e-stop so a\ntask that misses its deadline becomes a safe stop instead of a silent\nstall.\n\n## Supervision layers\nThree complementary fail-safe mechanisms, stacked:\n- **Vehicle state manager** (`fattos_vehicle`): the arming gate. Nothing\n  actuates unless `fattos_vehicle_can_actuate()` — ARMED with a healthy\n  supervisor. Arming requires the RC arm switch held for `arm_hold_ms`\n  with RC fresh, prearm true and the supervisor OK. RC loss or supervisor\n  fault drops to FAILSAFE (full re-arm required to recover); a latched\n  e-stop drops to ESTOP with explicit clear only.\n- **Task deadman** (`fattos_watchdog`): tasks feed within their timeout;\n  `fattos_wdt_kick_hardware()` forwards to the platform watchdog only when\n  all items are healthy, so a stalled task starves the HW watchdog.\n- **Actuator safety** (`fattos_safety`): each PWM channel must be\n  heartbeated; any timeout or a latched e-stop immediately writes zero duty\n  to every registered channel. Recovery is explicit\n  (`fattos_safety_estop_clear()`) and refused while channels are faulted.\n  Actuator commands go through `fattos_safety_command()`, which re-evaluates\n  state freshly on every write, clamps duty to [0, 1000], and forces zero\n  duty with `FATTOS_ERR_FAULT` while unhealthy — there is no way to write a\n  motor while the supervisor is faulted.\n\n## Autonomy layer\nAbove supervision, the autonomy stack turns sensor input into deliberate\nbehavior — all static, allocation-free, deterministic per tick:\n- **Behavior trees** (`fattos_bt`): the control task ticks a static tree;\n  composites (sequence/selector with resume memory, inverter) orchestrate\n  leaf actions and conditions over a caller-owned blackboard. Behaviors\n  are data, not scattered callback state.\n- **Motion profiles** (`fattos_motion`): trapezoidal accel-limited\n  references for point-to-point moves; retargetable mid-profile, never\n  overshoots, and the velocity reference feeds the PID feedforward term.\n- **RC input** (`fattos_rc`): SBUS stream -> 16 channels + failsafe flags,\n  feeding the vehicle arming gate.\n- **Ground-station commands** (`fattos_command`): typed request/reply over\n  the telemetry framing with explicit ack/nack counters.\n- **Logger** (`fattos_log`): leveled ring buffer, never blocks, overflow\n  counted; drain it to telemetry from a low-priority task.\n\n## IPC\nQueues are copy-based with fixed capacity and caller-provided storage;\nevents are 32-bit flag groups polled cooperatively; the pool is a\nfixed-block allocator whose free list lives inside free blocks. All three\nare O(1) and allocation-free.\n\n## AI integration boundary\nThe arena is static SRAM storage. Two backends exist:\n1. `fattos_nn`: the built-in INT8 dense runtime (see `docs/edge-ai.md`).\n   It executes inside the arena and reports real results.\n2. `FattosAIIO` seam: the single inference entry point. When a model is\n   bound it routes to the native runtime with exact shape checking; when\n   nothing is bound it returns `FATTOS_ERR_NOT_IMPLEMENTED` — an external\n   runtime (TensorFlow Lite Micro, CMSIS-NN) can be connected here later.\n\nDo not treat raw INT8 output as a motor command without model-specific\nquantization, calibration, bounds checks, and safety validation — the\nsafety supervisor must gate any learned policy's outputs.\n\n## Next architectural milestones\n1. MCU startup and board port (see `docs/porting-stm32.md`).\n2. Timer, GPIO, PWM, DMA, and sensor drivers.\n3. Cycle-counter WCET instrumentation below tick resolution.\n4. Optional preemptive scheduler with architecture-specific context switch.\n5. TensorFlow Lite Micro / CMSIS-NN integration behind the `FattosAIIO` seam\n   for models beyond the built-in runtime's scope.\n6. Multi-node transport (CAN/UART framing) on top of the telemetry codec.\n",
  "porting-stm32.md": "# STM32 port checklist\n\nThis starter does not contain a fake generic startup file because startup,\nclock setup, interrupt vectors, memory layout, and peripheral configuration\ndepend on the exact MCU and board.\n\n1. Use the matching vendor CMSIS device headers and startup assembly/C file.\n2. Add a linker script matching the selected Flash and SRAM sizes.\n3. Implement `fattos_platform_init()` to initialize board clocks and safe GPIO\n   states, or call it after vendor startup if clocks are configured there.\n4. Implement `fattos_platform_timer_start(FATTOS_TICK_HZ)` using a hardware\n   timer that fires at exactly `FATTOS_TICK_HZ` (default 1 kHz). The public\n   API is in milliseconds and converts with `fattos_ms_to_ticks()`, so any\n   tick rate works — but task and timeout resolution is one tick, so 1 kHz\n   is the recommended rate for control loops.\n5. In the timer ISR, call `fattos_tick_isr()` and clear the timer interrupt flag.\n6. Implement PWM with explicit channel bounds and `duty_permille <= 1000`.\n7. Implement `fattos_platform_irq_lock()/unlock()` as a true save-and-restore\n   of the interrupt state (read+clear PRIMASK, restore on unlock). Queues,\n   the memory pool, timer/watchdog registries and safety heartbeats depend\n   on it.\n8. Implement `fattos_platform_watchdog_kick()` to service an IWDG/WWDG and\n   ensure starving it resets the board.\n9. Add sensor drivers and error handling; do not use fake zero data in production.\n10. Measure callback execution time and worst-case interrupt latency and\n    compare against the scheduler's `exec_max_ticks`/`missed_releases`\n    counters on target.\n11. Connect `fattos_wdt_kick_hardware()` from a periodic task and add\n    independent hardware motor-disable circuitry.\n12. Add target-specific build and flashing instructions only after the board is known.\n\nDo not enable motors until timing, reset behavior, and emergency-stop behavior\nhave been tested on the actual hardware.\n",
  "edge-ai.md": "# Native edge AI on FattOS\n\n## What runs on the MCU\n`fattos_nn` is a small symmetric-INT8 neural-network interpreter that runs\ninside the static FattOS tensor arena (16 KiB default, `FATTOS_TENSOR_ARENA_SIZE`):\n\n- Ops: `FULLY_CONNECTED` (int8 weights, int32 bias, int32 accumulate) and\n  `RELU` (clamps to [0, 127] in symmetric quantization).\n- Per-layer requantization: `result = clamp8((acc * multiplier) >> shift)`.\n- Scratch: two int8 ping/pong buffers + one int32 accumulator row,\n  carved out of the arena at `fattos_nn_bind()`; the shape chain is\n  validated there (first bind failure wins, nothing is allocated).\n- Blocking and bounded: `fattos_nn_run()` executes in the calling task's\n  context with a fixed step count — no bytecode dispatch, no recursion.\n\n## Producing a model\nTrain anywhere (Keras, PyTorch, ONNX), then quantize a small MLP to\nsymmetric INT8 (zero point 0) and export per layer:\n\n1. `weights[out][in]` as `int8`, `bias[out]` as `int32` (bias scaled by\n   input_scale * weight_scale before the requant step).\n2. For each layer compute the float requant scale\n   `scale = (input_scale * weight_scale) / output_scale`.\n3. Convert to runtime parameters with\n   `fattos_nn_make_scale(scale, &multiplier, &shift)`, which solves\n   `scale ≈ multiplier / 2^shift`.\n4. Fill a `FattosNNLayer` per layer (op, pointers, feature counts,\n   multiplier, shift), then `FattosNNModel` and call `fattos_nn_bind()`.\n\nAt runtime quantize inputs with `fattos_nn_quantize_value()` (or\n`fattos_features_quantize()` for a feature vector) using the input scale\nyour toolchain reported, call `fattos_nn_run()`, and read class scores from\nthe output buffer; `fattos_nn_argmax()` gives the class decision.\n\n## Feature pipeline\n`fattos_features` turns raw sensor streams into those inputs: push samples\ninto a fixed rolling window (axes × length), then\n`fattos_features_vector()` produces `[mean(axes), rms(axes)]` ready for\nquantization. Typical setup: 32-sample × 3-axis window at 100 Hz →\n6-feature vector → quantize → `fattos_nn_run()`.\n\n## When to use something bigger\nThe runtime is intentionally dense-only. Choose TensorFlow Lite Micro or\nCMSIS-NN behind the `FattosAIIO` seam when you need convolutions, depthwise\nops, LSTM, or models whose scratch exceeds the arena. Keep the same rule\neither way: validate outputs (bounds, rate-of-change, fallback policy)\nbefore they can influence actuator commands, and let the safety supervisor\ngate the final outputs.\n",
  "autonomy.md": "# Building a robot application on FattOS\n\nThis is the recommended structure for an autonomous robot app — the same\none the host demo (`app/robot_main.c`) runs end-to-end. The layering is\nstrict: **sensors → fusion → vehicle gate → behavior → control → safety →\nactuators**. Each arrow is a hard boundary; nothing skips a layer.\n\n## 0. The board manifest — declare wiring once\n\n```c\nstatic const FattosDevice devices[] = {\n    {\"left_motor\",  FATTOS_DEV_MOTOR,    .pin = 0, .param = 60},\n    {\"right_motor\", FATTOS_DEV_MOTOR,    .pin = 1, .param = 60},\n    {\"drive\", FATTOS_DEV_ODOMETRY, .param = 1000,\n     .scale = 0.03f, .offset = 0.15f},\n    {\"imu\", FATTOS_DEV_IMU, .pin = 1},\n    {\"rc\",  FATTOS_DEV_RC,  .pin = 3},\n};\nstatic const FattosBoard board = {\"my-bot\", devices, 5};\n\nfattos_board_setup_safety(&board, &safety);  /* motors -> supervisor */\nfattos_board_setup_odometry(&board, &odom);  /* geometry -> odom */\nfattos_board_setup_rc(&board, &rc);\nfattos_board_setup_vehicle(&board, &vehicle, &safety, 100, 100);\n```\n\nEverything downstream uses names, not pins:\n`fattos_board_write_motor(&board, &safety, \"left_motor\", 400)` validates\nthe device kind, then routes through the supervisor's fresh health check,\nclamp, slew limiter and fail-safe. A manifest with no actuators is\nrejected at setup — wiring mistakes fail at startup, not on the road.\n\n## 1. The task layout\n\n| Task        | Rate  | Priority | Does                                              |\n|-------------|-------|----------|---------------------------------------------------|\n| rc_inject   | 20 ms | 4        | SBUS bytes -> `FattosRCState`                     |\n| imu_fusion  | 5 ms  | 3        | `fattos_imu_update_mag` + feature window push     |\n| control     | 10 ms | 5        | vehicle update, behavior tree tick, PID, profile, odometry, gated actuator writes, heartbeats |\n| nn_infer    | 50 ms | 2        | features -> quantize -> `fattos_ai_infer`         |\n| telemetry   | 100 ms| 1        | framed pose downlink, command registry dispatch   |\n| diagnostics | 100 ms| 1        | `fattos_safety_poll`, `fattos_wdt_poll`, HW kick  |\n| timers      | 10 ms | 1        | `fattos_timers_register` service task             |\n\nThe control task owns the loop; everything else feeds it or observes it.\n\n## 2. The vehicle gate — decide \"may we move?\" once\n\n```c\nFattosSafety safety;  fattos_safety_init(&safety);\nFattosVehicle vehicle; fattos_vehicle_init(&vehicle, &safety, 100, 100);\n\n/* every control cycle: */\nbool rc_ok = fattos_rc_signal_ok(&rc, fattos_millis(), 100);\nfattos_vehicle_set_rc(&vehicle, rc_ok, rc.channels[5] > 1300);\nfattos_vehicle_set_prearm(&vehicle, imu_converged && sensors_sane);\nfattos_vehicle_update(&vehicle);\nif (!fattos_vehicle_can_actuate(&vehicle)) { /* coast to zero, seed PID */ }\n```\n\nArming needs the switch held for `arm_hold_ms` with everything healthy.\nRC loss or a supervisor fault means FAILSAFE; recovery is always a full\nre-arm — never a silent jump back to ARMED.\n\n## 3. Behavior tree — what to do, as data\n\n```c\nFattosBTNode nodes[5] = {\n    {FATTOS_BT_SEQUENCE, 1, 1, 0, NULL, NULL},        /* root: cond then act */\n    {FATTOS_BT_INVERTER, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL},        /* obstacle? */\n    {FATTOS_BT_CONDITION, 3, 0, 0, cond_clear, NULL},\n    {FATTOS_BT_ACTION, -1, 0, 0, act_drive, NULL},\n    {FATTOS_BT_ACTION, -1, 0, 0, act_stop, NULL},     /* fallback */\n};\nFattosBT bt; fattos_bt_init(&bt, nodes, 5, &brain);   /* root = index 0 */\nfattos_bt_tick(&bt);                                  /* from control task */\n```\n\nComposites keep resume memory: a multi-tick ACTION returns\n`FATTOS_BT_RUNNING` and is resumed exactly where it left off next tick.\n\n## 3b. Go-to-goal navigation\n\n```c\nFattosNav nav; fattos_nav_init(&nav, 1.0f /*k*/, 0.4f /*max*/, 0.05f);\n/* each control cycle: */\nbool arrived = fattos_nav_step(&nav, odom.x, odom.y, odom.theta,\n                               goal_x, goal_y, &linear, &heading_target);\nfloat angular = fattos_pid_step_ff(&yaw_pid, heading_target, fused_yaw, dt, 0);\n```\n\nSpeed scales with remaining distance (smooth stop) and fades with heading\nerror (turn before you charge). Arrival inside the radius zeroes speed and\nincrements `goals_reached`.\n\n## 4. Motion profile — go somewhere without jerking\n\n```c\nFattosMotionProfile motion;\nfattos_motion_init(&motion, 0.4f /* m/s */, 0.5f /* m/s^2 */);\nfattos_motion_set_target(&motion, 0.35f);   /* legal mid-move too */\n/* each control cycle while driving: */\nfattos_motion_step(&motion, dt);\nlinear = motion.velocity;                    /* also your PID feedforward */\n```\n\n## 5. Control with feedforward and bumpless enable\n\n```c\nif (fattos_vehicle_can_actuate(&vehicle)) {\n    angular = fattos_pid_step_ff(&yaw_pid, target, fused_yaw, dt, 0.0f);\n} else {\n    /* Seed against the CURRENT measurement (zero effort), not the future\n       target — otherwise the integral parks at the anti-windup floor. */\n    fattos_pid_seed(&yaw_pid, fused_yaw, fused_yaw, 0.0f);\n}\nfattos_safety_command(&safety, left_id, duty_l);   /* never raw HAL */\n```\n\n## 6. Ground-station commands\n\nRegister typed handlers once; the dispatcher turns a telemetry frame into\nan ack/nack reply. Unknown ids and malformed payloads always get an\nexplicit answer with counters (`registry.handled/unknown/malformed`).\n\n## 7. Logging\n\n`fattos_log(&log, FATTOS_LOG_INFO, \"vehicle %s\", ...)` — never blocks,\nnever allocates; overflow drops oldest and counts it. Drain the newest\nentries to telemetry from the diagnostics task.\n\n## Rules that make this safe\n\n1. Only the control task writes actuators, and only via\n   `fattos_safety_command()`.\n2. Every actuator channel is heartbeated every control cycle; the\n   watchdog items mirror the tasks that must keep running.\n3. Register the overrun fault hook to `fattos_vehicle_estop()` — a task\n   that misses its deadline becomes a stop, not a surprise.\n4. Keep callbacks bounded and non-blocking; use `fattos_run_pending()`\n   with a budget in the main loop so nothing can spin forever.\n",
  "getting-started.md": "# Getting started with FattOS\n\nFattOS is a freestanding C11 OS foundation for robotics and autonomous MCU\napplications. Everything is static — no `malloc`, no heap, no hidden global\nstate in your application. If you can write a C struct and a function, you\ncan write FattOS code.\n\nThis guide takes you from zero to a complete, running control program.\n\n## Contents\n\n1. [Build the library (host)](#1-build-the-library-host)\n2. [Your first program](#2-your-first-program)\n3. [Add a sensor manifest](#3-add-a-sensor-manifest)\n4. [Add control and safety](#4-add-control-and-safety)\n5. [The run loop](#5-the-run-loop)\n6. [Next reading](#6-next-reading)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Build the library (host)\n\nRequirements: CMake 3.16+, a C11 compiler (GCC, Clang, or MSVC).\n\n```sh\ncmake -S . -B build -DFATTOS_BUILD_TESTS=ON -DFATTOS_BUILD_DEMO=ON\ncmake --build build\nctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure     # add \"-C Debug\" with the Visual Studio generator\n./build/fattos_demo                            # build\\Debug\\fattos_demo.exe on MSVC\n```\n\nLink your application against the `fattos` static target:\n\n```cmake\nadd_executable(my_robot app/main.c)\ntarget_link_libraries(my_robot PRIVATE fattos)\n```\n\nAll public headers live under `include/fattos/` and are included as:\n\n```c\n#include \"fattos/fattos.h\"         /* kernel */\n#include \"fattos/fattos_sensor.h\"  /* sensor subsystem */\n```\n\n## 2. Your first program\n\nA FattOS application is: initialize, register periodic tasks, then drain\ndue tasks in a bounded loop. There is no `main` framework — you own `main`.\n\n```c\n#include \"fattos/fattos.h\"\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nstatic void control_task(void *ctx)\n{\n    (void)ctx;\n    /* Runs every 10 ms. Bounded, non-blocking, returns promptly. */\n    uint32_t now = fattos_millis();\n    printf(\"tick %u\\n\", (unsigned)now);\n}\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    if (fattos_init() != FATTOS_OK) {\n        return 1;\n    }\n\n    /* The overrun fault hook: a late task becomes a stop, not a surprise. */\n    fattos_set_fault_hook(NULL); /* on a robot: call fattos_vehicle_estop() */\n\n    uint8_t control_id;\n    fattos_task_create(\"control\", control_task, NULL,\n                       10 /* ms */, 5 /* prio */, &control_id);\n\n    /* Bounded drain: never spins, never freezes. */\n    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) {\n        uint32_t dispatched = 0;\n        fattos_run_pending(8, &dispatched);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Rules that matter\n\n| Rule | Why |\n|---|---|\n| Callbacks are bounded and non-blocking | The scheduler is cooperative: one long callback delays every other task |\n| Durations are **milliseconds** | `FATTOS_TICK_HZ` is yours to choose; `fattos_ms_to_ticks()` converts |\n| Everything fallible returns `FattosStatus`; `FATTOS_OK == 0` | Check every return |\n| No dynamic allocation — ever | All storage is static or caller-provided |\n| Never call `fattos_run_once` from inside a callback | Re-entrancy is forbidden |\n\nOn real hardware your main loop becomes:\n\n```c\n_Noreturn void app_run(void)\n{\n    for (;;) {\n        bool ran = false;\n        fattos_run_once(&ran);\n        if (!ran) {\n            fattos_platform_idle();   /* sleep until the next tick */\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n…with a hardware timer ISR calling `fattos_tick_isr()` exactly\n`FATTOS_TICK_HZ` times per second.\n\n## 3. Add a sensor manifest\n\nDeclare **what is connected**, and the OS configures the chip, converts\nraw counts to SI units, paces reads at the requested rate, tracks health,\nand gates arming — no register pokes in application code.\n\n```c\n#include \"fattos/fattos_sensor.h\"\n#include \"fattos/fattos_bus.h\"\n\nstatic const FattosSensorEntry sensors[] = {\n    /* name    kind                    bus addr  rate  rangeG rangeDPS */\n    { \"imu\",   FATTOS_SENSOR_MPU6050,  0,  0x68, 200,  4.0f,  500.0f },\n    { \"mag\",   FATTOS_SENSOR_QMC5883,  0,  0x0D,  50,  0.0f,    0.0f },\n    { \"baro\",  FATTOS_SENSOR_BMP280,   0,  0x76,  25,  0.0f,    0.0f },\n    { \"tof\",   FATTOS_SENSOR_VL53L0X,  0,  0x29,  20,  0.0f,    0.0f },\n};\n```\n\nBind the manifest to a bus (on host, the simulated chip bus; on an MCU, your\nI2C port — same vtable):\n\n```c\nconst FattosBus *buses = fattos_sim_bus();      /* host/test */\nsize_t ok = fattos_sensor_setup(sensors, 4, buses, 1);\n/* ok == number of chips that probed and configured successfully.\n   A missing chip does not abort the others — prearm handles the policy. */\n```\n\nRead converted samples by name:\n\n```c\nFattosSensorSample s;\nif (fattos_sensor_read(\"imu\", &s) == FATTOS_OK) {\n    /* s.accel_g[3] in g, s.gyro_rads[3] in rad/s, s.temp_c in C */\n}\n```\n\nInside a periodic task, poll everything at once (each sensor only actually\nreads when its own rate is due):\n\n```c\nstatic void sensor_task(void *ctx)\n{\n    (void)ctx;\n    fattos_sensor_poll(fattos_millis());\n}\n```\n\nCalibrate the gyro at rest (before arming):\n\n```c\nfattos_sensor_calibrate(\"imu\", FATTOS_CAL_GYRO_BIAS, 200);\n/* ... poll until ... */\nif (fattos_sensor_cal_state(\"imu\") == FATTOS_CAL_DONE) { /* bias applied */ }\n```\n\nFull details, health semantics, and the driver catalog: **[docs/sensors.md](sensors.md)**.\n\n## 4. Add control and safety\n\nThree lines of policy make motion safe by default:\n\n```c\n/* 1. Actuator supervisor: every motor channel gets a heartbeat timeout. */\nFattosSafety safety;\nfattos_safety_init(&safety);\nuint8_t left;\nfattos_safety_register_channel(&safety, \"left_motor\", 0, 60 /* ms */, &left);\n\n/* 2. Vehicle gate: deliberate arming, failsafe on RC loss, latched e-stop. */\nFattosVehicle veh;\nfattos_vehicle_init(&veh, &safety, 100 /* arm hold ms */, 100 /* RC timeout */);\n\n/* 3. Prearm joins sensor health automatically: if a manifest exists, every\n      sensor must be present, fresh and plausible to arm. */\nfattos_vehicle_set_rc(&veh, true, arm_switch);\n\nfor (;;) {\n    fattos_vehicle_update(&veh);\n    if (fattos_vehicle_can_actuate(&veh)) {\n        fattos_safety_heartbeat(&safety, left);\n        fattos_safety_command(&safety, left, 400 /* permille */);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Never** call `fattos_platform_pwm_write()` from application code. Every\nmotor command goes through `fattos_safety_command()` (or\n`fattos_board_write_motor()`), which re-checks health freshly, clamps to\n[0, 1000], applies slew limiting, and forces zero duty with\n`FATTOS_ERR_FAULT` whenever the supervisor is unhealthy.\n\nMore: **[docs/safety.md](safety.md)**.\n\n## 5. The run loop\n\nThe canonical task layout (from the reference application):\n\n| task | rate | priority | role |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| sensors | 5 ms | 4 | `fattos_sensor_poll` |\n| imu fusion | 5 ms | 3 | `fattos_imu_update_mag` |\n| control | 10 ms | 5 | vehicle → nav → BT → PID → mixer → heartbeats → gated writes |\n| diagnostics | 100 ms | 1 | `fattos_safety_poll`, `fattos_wdt_poll`, hardware watchdog |\n| telemetry | 100 ms | 1 | apply received commands, send status frames |\n\nPriorities: **higher number wins** among due tasks. Keep the control loop\nstrictly highest; give I/O and telemetry the lowest.\n\n## 6. Next reading\n\n| You want to… | Read |\n|---|---|\n| Understand the kernel, timers, queues, watchdog | [docs/kernel.md](kernel.md) |\n| Wire sensors and add new chips | [docs/sensors.md](sensors.md), [docs/writing-drivers.md](writing-drivers.md) |\n| Build safe actuation | [docs/safety.md](safety.md) |\n| Write control loops and navigation | [docs/control.md](control.md) |\n| Talk to a ground station | [docs/communications.md](communications.md) |\n| Port to an MCU | [docs/porting-stm32.md](porting-stm32.md) |\n| Look up any function signature | [docs/api-reference.md](api-reference.md) |\n| Give an AI agent full context | [AGENT.md](../AGENT.md) |",
  "kernel.md": "# The FattOS kernel\n\nThe kernel is two things: a **clock** and a **cooperative, fixed-priority\nscheduler**, plus a set of allocation-free IPC/instrumentation services\nbuilt on top of them.\n\nContents:\n- [Concepts](#concepts)\n- [The scheduler](#the-scheduler)\n- [Task profiling and the fault hook](#task-profiling-and-the-fault-hook)\n- [Quarantine: self-healing under overruns](#quarantine-self-healing-under-overruns)\n- [Queues](#queues)\n- [Software timers](#software-timers)\n- [Event flags](#event-flags)\n- [Memory pool](#memory-pool)\n- [Deadman watchdog](#deadman-watchdog)\n- [Critical sections and ISR rules](#critical-sections-and-isr-rules)\n- [Configuration knobs](#configuration-knobs)\n\nHeader: `fattos/fattos.h`, `fattos/fattos_config.h`, plus\n`fattos_queue.h / fattos_timer.h / fattos_event.h / fattos_mem.h / fattos_watchdog.h`.\n\n---\n\n## Concepts\n\n- **Tick** — the scheduler's heartbeat. `FATTOS_TICK_HZ` ticks happen per\n  second (default 1000), driven by `fattos_tick_isr()` from a hardware timer\n  ISR on target, or `fattos_host_advance(n)` in host tests. Public APIs take\n  **milliseconds**; `fattos_ms_to_ticks(ms)` converts with round-up and never\n  returns 0 for a non-zero duration.\n- **Task** — a periodic callback with a period, a priority, and an optional\n  context pointer. Tasks never \"block\": there is no semaphore to wait on by\n  design. A task does its work and returns; if it can't do work this cycle,\n  it returns early.\n- **Due** — `now >= next_release`. After being dispatched, the task's\n  `next_release` advances by whole periods, so a task that is late does not\n  replay a burst of missed periods (misses are counted instead).\n- **Millisecond clock** — `fattos_millis()` wraps safely at 2^32 ms\n  (~49.7 days). Always compare with the `(int32_t)(now - deadline) < 0`\n  idiom (the kernel does this internally).\n\n---\n\n## The scheduler\n\n```c\nFattosStatus fattos_init(void);\n\nFattosStatus fattos_task_create(const char *name, FattosTaskFn fn,\n                                void *context, uint32_t period_ms,\n                                uint8_t priority, uint8_t *out_task_id);\n\nuint32_t fattos_millis(void);\nuint32_t fattos_ms_to_ticks(uint32_t ms);\nvoid     fattos_tick_isr(void);            /* exactly FATTOS_TICK_HZ / s */\n```\n\nDispatch:\n\n```c\nFattosStatus fattos_run_once(bool *ran);                    /* one callback */\nFattosStatus fattos_run_pending(uint32_t budget,\n                                uint32_t *dispatched);      /* bounded drain */\n_Noreturn void fattos_run(void);                            /* run forever  */\n```\n\nProperties you can rely on:\n\n1. **Highest numeric priority first** among all due tasks.\n2. **Fair ties** — equal-priority due tasks rotate through the scan order\n   (`g_scan_cursor`), so no task is systematically last every cycle.\n3. **One callback per `fattos_run_once`** — your main loop stays in control.\n4. **Bounded drain** — `fattos_run_pending(budget, …)` runs at most `budget`\n   callbacks and reports how many ran. A runaway application callback can\n   never turn a drain loop into an infinite spin.\n5. **No due task is not an error** — `FATTOS_OK` with `*ran == false`.\n\n```c\n/* MCU main loop */\nfor (;;) {\n    bool ran = false;\n    fattos_run_once(&ran);\n    if (!ran) fattos_platform_idle();\n}\n\n/* Host / cooperative integration into someone else's loop */\nuint32_t n = 0;\nfattos_run_pending(8, &n);   /* run at most 8 due callbacks */\n```\n\nEnable/disable:\n\n```c\nfattos_task_enable(id, false);   /* pause; re-enabling restarts cleanly:\n                                    next_release = now (no backlog replay) */\nsize_t n = fattos_task_count();  /* slots used */\n```\n\nTasks can be **disabled while quarantined** — see below.\n\n---\n\n## Task profiling and the fault hook\n\nEvery task carries a `FattosTaskInfo`:\n\n```c\ntypedef struct {\n    const char *name;\n    FattosTaskFn function;\n    void *context;\n    uint32_t period_ticks, next_release, run_count;\n    uint8_t priority;\n    bool enabled;\n    uint32_t exec_last_ticks;    /* last execution time, tick resolution */\n    uint32_t exec_max_ticks;     /* WCET observed so far */\n    uint32_t missed_releases;    /* periods that elapsed without dispatch */\n    uint32_t overrun_count;      /* executions longer than the period */\n    uint32_t jitter_max_ticks;   /* worst lateness: due time -> dispatch */\n    uint32_t overrun_streak;     /* consecutive overruns since a clean run */\n    bool quarantined;            /* auto-disabled by sustained overruns */\n} FattosTaskInfo;\n```\n\n- `exec_max_ticks` versus your period is the **schedulability check**: if\n  `exec_max + jitter_max ≥ period`, the schedule is infeasible at that load.\n- `jitter_max_ticks` is your real scheduling-latency WCET on target.\n- Sub-tick executions read as 0; use a cycle counter in the port if you need\n  sub-millisecond WCET.\n\n```c\nFattosTaskInfo info;\nfattos_task_get_info(id, &info);\n```\n\n**Fault hook** — called *in task context, interrupts enabled*, right after\na callback overruns:\n\n```c\nvoid on_overrun(uint8_t task_id, uint32_t exec_ticks, uint32_t period_ticks)\n{\n    (void)exec_ticks; (void)period_ticks;\n    /* Safety-first: a late task becomes a stop, not a surprise. */\n    fattos_vehicle_estop(&vehicle);\n}\nfattos_set_fault_hook(on_overrun);\n```\n\n---\n\n## Quarantine: self-healing under overruns\n\nThe cooperative scheduler cannot preempt a bad callback — so it **isolates**\nit instead. This is the kernel turning the classic RTOS \"one task can sink\nthe ship\" limitation into a managed capability:\n\n- Each task counts `overrun_streak`: overruns in a row since its last clean\n  run. A single clean run resets the streak — transient spikes are forgiven.\n- When the streak reaches `FATTOS_QUARANTINE_LIMIT` (default **3**,\n  configurable in `fattos_config.h`), the task is **quarantined**: flagged\n  and disabled. The rest of the system keeps its deadlines.\n- Quarantine still calls the fault hook on each overrun, so your e-stop\n  policy runs first, isolation second.\n- A quarantined task **cannot be re-enabled by `fattos_task_enable`** —\n  that returns `FATTOS_ERR_FAULT`. Only an explicit rehabilitation says\n  \"we looked at it, it is fixed\":\n\n```c\nif (fattos_task_is_quarantined(id)) {\n    /* inspect: why did it overrun? fix, then release it. */\n    fattos_task_rehab(id);       /* clears streak, re-enables fresh */\n}\nsize_t isolated = fattos_task_quarantined_count();   /* for telemetry */\n```\n\nDesign rule: `fattos_set_fault_hook` + vehicle e-stop handles the *safety*\nresponse; quarantine handles the *availability* response. Use both.\n\n---\n\n## Queues\n\nFixed-capacity, copy-in/copy-out, caller-provided storage. Internally\nprotected by `fattos_platform_irq_lock()`, so they are valid ISR→task\nchannels on ports that implement the irq lock properly.\n\n```c\ntypedef struct { /* ... */ } FattosQueue;\n\nstatic uint8_t q_storage[8 * sizeof(float)];\n\nFattosQueue q;\nfattos_queue_init(&q, q_storage, sizeof(float), 8);\n\nfattos_queue_send(&q, &value);        /* FATTOS_ERR_FULL when full   */\nfattos_queue_receive(&q, &out);       /* FATTOS_ERR_EMPTY when empty */\nfattos_queue_peek(&q, &out);          /* copy without consuming      */\nsize_t n    = fattos_queue_count(&q);\nbool empty  = fattos_queue_is_empty(&q);\nbool full   = fattos_queue_is_full(&q);\n```\n\nGuidance:\n- Never loop waiting for space — check the return and move on (or drop the\n  oldest by receiving first, if your protocol tolerates it).\n- `item_size` is bytes; the storage must hold `item_size * capacity` bytes.\n\n---\n\n## Software timers\n\nStatic one-shot or auto-reload timers, serviced by whichever task calls\n`fattos_timer_service()`. Missed periods are **skipped, not burst**.\n\n```c\nstatic FattosTimer blink;\n\nfattos_timer_init(&blink, \"blink\", on_blink, NULL);\nfattos_timer_start(&blink, 500 /* ms */, true /* autoreload */);\nfattos_timer_stop(&blink);\nbool active = fattos_timer_is_active(&blink);\n```\n\nConvenience: create the service task once at startup —\n\n```c\nuint8_t tid;\nfattos_timers_register(10 /* service period ms */, 1, &tid);\n```\n\nTimer callbacks run in the service task's context: keep them short,\nnon-blocking — same rules as task callbacks. `fattos_timer_count()`\nreports registered timers.\n\n---\n\n## Event flags\n\n32-bit flag groups with **non-blocking** semantics (a cooperative scheduler\ncannot block a task):\n\n```c\nstatic FattosEventGroup ev;\nfattos_event_init(&ev);\n\nfattos_event_set(&ev, 1u << 3);      /* producer (task or ISR)     */\nfattos_event_clear(&ev,1u << 3);\nif (fattos_event_poll(&ev, 1u << 3, false)) { ... }   /* ANY of bits */\nif (fattos_event_poll(&ev, 0x5u, true))       { ... } /* ALL of bits */\nuint32_t current = fattos_event_get(&ev);\n```\n\nPattern: producer sets bits; consumer polls each cycle and continues when\nthe poll returns false. Nothing ever waits.\n\n---\n\n## Memory pool\n\nFixed-block allocator: O(1) alloc/free, zero fragmentation, free list lives\ninside the free blocks themselves. ISR-safe via critical sections.\n\n```c\nstatic uint8_t pool_storage[16 * 128];\n\nFattosPool pool;\nfattos_pool_init(&pool, pool_storage, 128 /* block size */, 16);\n\nvoid *block = fattos_pool_alloc(&pool);   /* NULL when exhausted */\nfattos_pool_free(&pool, block);\nsize_t free_n = fattos_pool_free_count(&pool);\n```\n\nRules: every block must be pointer-aligned and ≥ `sizeof(void*)` bytes\n(the free list is threaded through free blocks). Allocation failure is a\nnormal, expected condition — handle NULL.\n\n---\n\n## Deadman watchdog\n\nEach registered item must be fed within its timeout. `fattos_wdt_poll()`\nre-evaluates all items; **`fattos_wdt_kick_hardware()` only kicks the\nhardware watchdog while every item is healthy**, so a stalled task starves\nthe IWDG and the MCU resets itself.\n\n```c\nuint8_t control_wdt;\nfattos_wdt_register(\"control\", 50 /* ms */, &control_wdt);\n\n/* in the control task, every cycle: */\nfattos_wdt_feed(control_wdt);\n\n/* in the diagnostics task: */\nif (fattos_wdt_poll() != FATTOS_OK) {\n    /* which item died? */\n    uint8_t victim = fattos_wdt_fault_id();\n}\nfattos_wdt_kick_hardware();   /* no-op while any item is expired */\n```\n\nAPI: `fattos_wdt_poll()` (`FATTOS_OK` healthy / `FATTOS_ERR_TIMEOUT`),\n`fattos_wdt_is_healthy()`, `fattos_wdt_fault_id()` (`0xFF` = none),\n`fattos_wdt_count()`, `fattos_wdt_get_info(id, &item)`.\n\nLayer the soft deadman (this) under the hardware watchdog: soft catches\n*slow* stalls with a diagnosable name; hardware catches a hard scheduler\nhang no software can see.\n\n---\n\n## Critical sections and ISR rules\n\n```c\nuint32_t key = fattos_platform_irq_lock();\n/* ... critical section ... */\nfattos_platform_irq_unlock(key);\n```\n\n- MCU ports must save/restore full interrupt state (e.g. PRIMASK).\n- Never allocate the key yourself; never unlock with a different key.\n- ISRs should only: call `fattos_tick_isr()`, push bytes into queues, and\n  set event bits. **Never call task functions, timers, or the logger's\n  printf path from an ISR.**\n\n---\n\n## Configuration knobs\n\nAll in `fattos/fattos_config.h` (override with compiler `-D`):\n\n| Macro | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `FATTOS_MAX_TASKS` | 8 | task table size |\n| `FATTOS_TICK_HZ` | 1000 | tick rate; public durations stay in ms |\n| `FATTOS_MAX_TIMERS` | 8 | software timer registry |\n| `FATTOS_MAX_WDT_ITEMS` | 8 | deadman items |\n| `FATTOS_QUARANTINE_LIMIT` | 3 | consecutive overruns before isolation |\n| `FATTOS_TENSOR_ARENA_SIZE` | 16384 | static AI arena bytes |\n| `FATTOS_TENSOR_ARENA_ALIGNMENT` | 16 | arena alignment |\n\nZero values for tasks/timers/wdt/tick are compile-time errors, so a bad\nconfiguration fails the build, not the flight.\n\n---\n\n## Checklist: writing a good task\n\n1. Period and priority chosen from the control requirements, not guessed.\n2. Bounded work — worst-case path returns quickly; no waiting, no I/O\n   stalls, no `printf` in the hot path (log into the ring instead).\n3. Reads sensors by name (`fattos_sensor_read`), computes, writes actuators\n   through the safety supervisor, feeds its own watchdog item, returns.\n4. Fault-hook registered so its own overruns e-stop instead of degrading.\n5. `exec_max + jitter_max < period` verified on target before shipping.",
  "sensors.md": "# Sensors & peripheral drivers\n\n> **Declare the part. The OS knows the part.**\n\nA FattOS board manifest says *what* chip sits *where*, at *what rate*.\nThe sensor subsystem does everything else: binds the right driver, probes\nWHO_AM_I, programs the chip from plain parameters (data rate, full-scale\nrange), paces reads into rate groups, converts raw counts into SI units,\nruns auto-calibration, checks physical plausibility, tracks health, and\ngates vehicle arming. Application code never touches a chip register.\n\nHeaders: `fattos/fattos_sensor.h`, `fattos/fattos_bus.h`\nSources: `robot/fattos_sensor.c`, `robot/fattos_bus.c`, `robot/fattos_fusion.c`\nHost simulator: `platform/host/fattos_sim_bus.{h,c}`\n\nContents:\n1. [The manifest](#1-the-manifest)\n2. [The bus HAL](#2-the-bus-hal)\n3. [Setup: what happens at boot](#3-setup-what-happens-at-boot)\n4. [Reading sensors](#4-reading-sensors)\n5. [Rate groups: `fattos_sensor_poll`](#5-rate-groups-fattos_sensor_poll)\n6. [Health model](#6-health-model)\n7. [Auto-calibration](#7-auto-calibration)\n8. [Driver catalog](#8-driver-catalog)\n9. [Deriving everything: heading, altitude, climb rate](#9-deriving-everything-heading-altitude-climb-rate)\n10. [Arming gate](#10-arming-gate)\n11. [Diagnostics & telemetry](#11-diagnostics--telemetry)\n12. [Testing on the host simulator](#12-testing-on-the-host-simulator)\n13. [Adding a new chip](#13-adding-a-new-chip)\n\n---\n\n## 1. The manifest\n\n```c\n#include \"fattos/fattos_sensor.h\"\n\n/* name, kind, bus index, 7-bit addr, ODR Hz, accel range g, gyro range dps */\nstatic const FattosSensorEntry sensors[] = {\n    { \"imu\",  FATTOS_SENSOR_MPU6050,  0, 0x68, 200, 4.0f,  500.0f },\n    { \"mag\",  FATTOS_SENSOR_QMC5883,  0, 0x0D,  50, 0.0f,    0.0f },\n    { \"baro\", FATTOS_SENSOR_BMP280,   0, 0x76,  25, 0.0f,    0.0f },\n    { \"tof\",  FATTOS_SENSOR_VL53L0X,  0, 0x29,  20, 0.0f,    0.0f },\n};\n```\n\nRules:\n\n- `name` — unique string; it is the handle used by\n  `fattos_sensor_read(\"imu\", ...)`, calibration, and telemetry lookups.\n- `kind` — one of the `FattosSensorKind` values (the driver catalog below).\n  The subsystem maps kind → driver automatically; an unknown kind simply\n  stays `MISSING`.\n- `bus` — index into the bus table handed to `fattos_sensor_setup`.\n- `addr7` — 7-bit I2C address (write it the way the datasheet prints it:\n  0x68, not 0xD0).\n- `rate_hz` — desired output data rate. The driver picks the nearest legal\n  chip setting ≤ this rate, and the subsystem uses `1000/rate` ms as the\n  read period.\n- `range_g` / `range_dps` — IMU full-scale selection (`0` = driver\n  default: ±2 g / ±250 dps). Bigger range → bigger range before\n  saturation, coarser quantization. Choose the smallest range your\n  dynamics need.\n\nUp to `FATTOS_MAX_SENSORS` (default 8) entries. The manifest is plain\ndata — grep-able, diff-able, and exactly what an AI agent needs to know a\nrobot's sensor payloads without reading schematics.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The bus HAL\n\nDrivers never call the platform HAL directly. They talk to a `FattosBus` —\na name plus a vtable. One bus instance per physical bus; a board may mix\nseveral I2C buses, an SPI bus, a UART, and a CAN bus without globals.\n\n```c\ntypedef struct {\n    /* Combined I2C transaction: write wr_len, repeated-start read rd_len.\n       This is the register-access pattern every sensor uses. */\n    FattosStatus (*i2c_write_read)(void *ctx, uint8_t addr7,\n                                   const uint8_t *wr, size_t wr_len,\n                                   uint8_t *rd, size_t rd_len);\n    FattosStatus (*spi_transfer)(void *ctx, uint16_t cs_pin,\n                                 const uint8_t *tx, uint8_t *rx, size_t len);\n    FattosStatus (*uart_write)(void *ctx, uint8_t port,\n                               const uint8_t *buf, size_t len);\n    int  (*uart_read)(void *ctx, uint8_t port, uint8_t *buf, size_t cap);\n    FattosStatus (*can_send)(void *ctx, uint32_t id,\n                             const uint8_t *data, size_t len);\n    int  (*can_recv)(void *ctx, uint32_t *id, uint8_t *data, size_t cap);\n    void *ctx;   /* bus handle passed to every op */\n} FattosBusOps;\n\ntypedef struct {\n    const char *name;        /* \"i2c0\" */\n    const FattosBusOps *ops; /* NULL = bus not present at all */\n} FattosBus;\n```\n\nHelper wrappers (`fattos_bus.h`), all NULL-safe:\n\n```c\nfattos_i2c_write(bus, addr7, data, len);\nfattos_i2c_read(bus, addr7, reg, buf, len);\nfattos_i2c_write_u8(bus, addr7, reg, value);\nfattos_i2c_read_u8(bus, addr7, reg, &value);\nfattos_spi_transfer(bus, cs_pin, tx, rx, len);\nfattos_uart_write(bus, port, buf, len);\nint n = fattos_uart_read(bus, port, buf, cap);\nfattos_can_send(bus, id, data, len);     /* len ≤ 8 */\nint m = fattos_can_recv(bus, &id, buf, cap);\n```\n\nContract for port authors:\n\n- Return `FATTOS_OK` or an error code — never partial silent success.\n- `i2c_write_read` must be a genuine combined transaction (repeated\n  start), because some chips refuse separate write/read sequences.\n- A missing bus class or absent bus returns `FATTOS_ERR_NOT_IMPLEMENTED`\n  (helpers) or `FATTOS_ERR_PLATFORM` (NACK) — a misconfigured manifest\n  fails loudly at the first read instead of returning phantom data.\n- Bus ops may be called from a task context only (the sensor subsystem is\n  not ISR-safe by design — reads happen in the polling task).\n\n### Porting a real I2C peripheral\n\n```c\nstatic FattosStatus board_i2c_write_read(void *ctx, uint8_t addr7,\n        const uint8_t *wr, size_t wr_len, uint8_t *rd, size_t rd_len)\n{\n    I2C_Handle *i2c = ctx;\n    /* 1. START, addr<<1|W, write wr_len bytes\n       2. repeated START, addr<<1|R, read rd_len bytes\n       3. map NACK/timeouts -> FATTOS_ERR_PLATFORM / FATTOS_ERR_TIMEOUT */\n}\n\nstatic const FattosBusOps board_i2c0_ops = {\n    .i2c_write_read = board_i2c_write_read,\n    .ctx = &hi2c1,\n};\nstatic const FattosBus board_buses[] = {\n    { \"i2c0\", &board_i2c0_ops },\n};\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Setup: what happens at boot\n\n```c\nsize_t ok = fattos_sensor_setup(sensors, 4, board_buses, 1);\n```\n\nPer entry, the subsystem:\n\n1. Validates kind/bus/name limits.\n2. Reads the chip's **WHO_AM_I** register through the driver.\n3. If and only if the ID matches the expected silicon, runs the driver's\n   **configuration**: wake/reset, choose nearest-legal ODR and full-scale,\n   read factory calibration (BMP280), start continuous measurement.\n4. Sets the sensor STALE (present, awaiting its first sample) and arms its\n   rate-group deadline.\n\nFailure handling is deliberately non-fatal per entry:\n\n| Outcome at setup | Health | Effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| Probe matches + config OK | `STALE` → `OK` at first sample | counted in the return value |\n| No ACK / wrong WHO_AM_I | `MISSING` | prearm will refuse to arm |\n| Unknown kind, bad bus index | `MISSING` | reported, others continue |\n\n**One bad part must not brick the robot** — prearm policy decides whether\nit may fly, but a missing compass chip still lets you boot, log, and\ndiagnose. The return value is the number of sensors that came up OK.\n\nCall `fattos_sensor_setup` once at boot, after `fattos_init()` and after\nthe buses are initialized. Calling it again resets all sensor state\n(it performs `fattos_sensor_reset()` first).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Reading sensors\n\n```c\nFattosSensorSample s;\nFattosStatus st = fattos_sensor_read(\"imu\", &s);\nif (st == FATTOS_OK) {\n    /* fresh, plausible data */\n} else if (st == FATTOS_ERR_TIMEOUT) {\n    /* stale or faulted: s still holds the LAST GOOD sample */\n} else { /* FATTOS_ERR_INVALID_ARG: unknown name (or NULL out) */ }\n```\n\n`FattosSensorSample` (SI units, ready to use):\n\n```c\ntypedef struct {\n    uint32_t timestamp_ms;   /* when the sample was taken */\n    uint8_t  valid;           /* bitmask of populated fields:\n                                 1=accel 2=gyro 4=mag 8=pressure 16=temp 32=range */\n    float accel_g[3];         /* accelerometer, g          */\n    float gyro_rads[3];       /* gyroscope, rad/s          */\n    float mag_ut[3];          /* magnetometer, microtesla  */\n    float pressure_pa;        /* barometric pressure, Pa   */\n    float temp_c;             /* die temperature, °C       */\n    float range_m;            /* rangefinder distance, m   */\n} FattosSensorSample;\n```\n\nAlways check `s.valid` before consuming a field — a barometer never\npopulates `accel_g`, and the bitmask exists so generic code can handle\nany payload.\n\nThe `timestamp_ms` lets your own logic detect staleness independently of\nthe built-in health model (e.g. fusing with `dt = now - s.timestamp`).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Rate groups: `fattos_sensor_poll`\n\nReading a sensor does not care what time it is. *Polling* does:\n\n```c\nstatic void sensor_task(void *ctx)\n{\n    (void)ctx;\n    size_t n = fattos_sensor_poll(fattos_millis()); /* how many were due */\n    (void)n;\n}\n```\n\n- Each sensor only performs bus I/O when `now >= next_due`, where its\n  period is `1000/rate_hz` ms (min 1 ms). Sensors with different rates\n  form natural **rate groups**: at 100 Hz the IMU gets read every poll,\n  the barometer once every 4th poll at 25 Hz — no code, just math.\n- The return value is the number of sensors actually read this call —\n  useful as a scheduling sanity check.\n- Pacing is based on the `now_ms` you pass in; use `fattos_millis()`, or a\n  simulated time in host tests (`fattos_host_advance`).\n- After a failed read the sensor is rescheduled normally (errors do not\n  busy-retry; they follow the rate anyway).\n\nFailure inside a poll: the error counter increments; after\n`5` consecutive bus errors the health becomes `ERROR`; a single glitch\nbetween healthy reads just marks the sensor temporarily `STALE` until the\nnext good read.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Health model\n\n```c\ntypedef enum {\n    FATTOS_SENSOR_OK = 0,      /* present, fresh, plausible */\n    FATTOS_SENSOR_MISSING,     /* not in manifest / probe failed */\n    FATTOS_SENSOR_ERROR,       /* 5+ consecutive bus errors */\n    FATTOS_SENSOR_STALE,       /* no good sample within 3x its period */\n    FATTOS_SENSOR_PLAUSIBLE    /* sample outside physical bounds */\n} FattosSensorHealth;\n```\n\n`fattos_sensor_health_name()` maps these to strings for logs.\n\nWhat each state means operationally:\n\n- **MISSING** — WHO_AM_I failed at boot (wrong chip, wiring fault, wrong\n  address, bus not initialized). Persists until re-setup/replug.\n- **ERROR** — the chip was there but the bus keeps failing now. If it\n  recovers, the very next good read returns health to `OK`.\n- **STALE** — the chip is alive but the data is too old. Also the state\n  between setup and the first sample, and the transient state while\n  errors accumulate.\n- **PLAUSIBLE** (naming: \"failed plausibility\") — a sample arrived that\n  violates physics; it is **discarded** (the last good sample stays), and\n  health flips until a sane sample appears.\n\nPlausibility envelopes (deliberately generous; they catch dead/wrong-\nprotocol parts, not vigorous maneuvers):\n\n| Signal | Accepted range | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| accel magnitude | ≥ 0.02 g, ≤ `range_g × 1.02` | a constant 0 output or rail-high output is a wiring/protocol failure |\n| pressure | 30 000 … 120 000 Pa | covers altitudes −500 m … +9 000 m |\n| temperature | −40 … +125 °C | silicon ratings |\n| range | > 0 … 4 m | ToF sensor physics |\n\nStaleness sweep: any `OK` sensor with no good sample for **3× its period**\nis marked `STALE` by the next poll even without an error.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Auto-calibration\n\nThe one calibration every IMU needs — gyro bias at rest — is built in:\n\n```c\n/* at boot, vehicle still on the ground: */\nfattos_sensor_calibrate(\"imu\", FATTOS_CAL_GYRO_BIAS, 200);\n```\n\n- The driver averages `samples` gyro reads while you keep polling; the\n  mean becomes a per-sensor bias subtracted from every subsequent\n  `gyro_rads` output. State machine: `FATTOS_CAL_IDLE →\n  FATTOS_CAL_GYRO_BIAS → FATTOS_CAL_DONE` (or `FATTOS_CAL_FAILED` with an\n  unknown name / non-IMU sensor / NULL).\n- Poll `fattos_sensor_cal_state(\"imu\")` from a boot/wait task; do not arm\n  until `FATTOS_CAL_DONE`.\n- The bias survives into normal samples transparently — consumers keep\n  reading `gyro_rads` and it is already corrected.\n- Calibration stops automatically when its sample count completes; it\n  never runs unbounded. Reset with `FATTOS_CAL_IDLE`.\n\n```c\nif (fattos_sensor_calibrate(\"imu\", FATTOS_CAL_GYRO_BIAS, 400) != FATTOS_OK) {\n    /* unknown name, or this sensor kind does not support it */\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8. Driver catalog\n\nEvery driver implements: WHO_AM_I probe → configuration from manifest\nparameters → SI-unit conversion → the health/plausibility rules above.\n\n### `FATTOS_SENSOR_MPU6050` — 6-axis IMU (I2C, accel + gyro + temp)\n\n- Probe: register `0x75` must read `0x68`.\n- Config: wakes from sleep (internal 8 MHz PLL, `PWR_MGMT_1 = 0x01`),\n  `SMPLRT_DIV = 1000/rate − 1`, nearest-legal ACCEL_CONFIG/GYRO_CONFIG\n  full-scale selectors from `range_g` / `range_dps`.\n- Output: `accel_g[3]`, `gyro_rads[3]`, `temp_c` (datasheet formula\n  `raw/340 + 36.53`), `valid |= 0x01|0x02|0x10`.\n- Scaling: `fs_g/32768` g per LSB at selected range (2/4/8/16 g),\n  `fs_dps·π/180/32768` rad/s per LSB (250/500/1000/2000 dps).\n- Supports auto-calibration of gyro bias.\n\n### `FATTOS_SENSOR_QMC5883` — 3-axis magnetometer (I2C)\n\n- Probe: register `0x0D` reads `0xFF` (5883L) or `0x01` (5883).\n- Config: soft reset (`0x0A ← 0x80`), set/reset period enabled\n  (`0x0B ← 0x01`), ODR selected from `rate_hz` (10/50/100/200 Hz),\n  ±2 gauss full scale with maximum oversampling (OSR 512).\n- Output: `mag_ut[3]` in microtesla at 12 LSB/µT, `valid |= 0x04`.\n- Typical use: feed `mag_ut` into `fattos_imu_update_mag()` for absolute\n  heading (see [docs/control.md](control.md)).\n\n### `FATTOS_SENSOR_BMP280` — barometer + temperature (I2C)\n\n- Probe: register `0xD0` reads `0x58`.\n- Config: soft reset (`0xE0 ← 0xB6`), reads the 24-byte factory\n  calibration block at `0x88`, chooses the IIR filter coefficient from\n  `rate_hz` (faster ODR → less filtering), starts normal mode with ×16\n  pressure / ×2 temperature oversampling.\n- Output: `pressure_pa`, `temp_c` via the **datasheet integer\n  compensation** exactly as published (also shared with the host\n  emulator through `fattos_sensor_internal.h` so tests encode raw counts\n  that mean a real pressure), `valid |= 0x08|0x10`.\n\n### `FATTOS_SENSOR_VL53L0X` — time-of-flight rangefinder (I2C)\n\n- Probe: model ID register `0xC0` reads `0xEE`.\n- Config: boots the ranging state machine (sequence of single-register\n  writes matching the ST basic init) and sets a ~33 ms timing budget.\n- Output: `range_m` from the 16-bit millimeter reading at `0x1E`,\n  `valid |= 0x20`.\n- Typical use: tilt-corrected height into `FattosFusionInput.range_m`\n  with `cos_tilt` from your attitude estimate.\n\n### Not-yet-ported parts\n\nA kind not in the enum is simply reported `MISSING`. The contract for\nadding one is mechanical — see\n[docs/writing-drivers.md](writing-drivers.md); it is: one enum value,\nthree functions (probe/configure/read), one row in the driver tables.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Deriving everything: heading, altitude, climb rate\n\nThe subsystem turns raw payload bytes into the quantities an autonomous\nvehicle actually needs.\n\n### Absolute heading (mag + accel → yaw)\n\n```c\nFattosSensorSample imu, mag;\nif (fattos_sensor_read(\"imu\", &imu) == FATTOS_OK &&\n    fatostrue_read(\"mag\", &mag) == FATTOS_OK) {\n    fatostrue_update_mag(&state, imu.gyro_rads, imu.accel_g,\n                        mag.mag_ut, dt);\n}\n```\n\n(Exact call: `fattos_imu_update_mag(&imu_state, gyro, accel, mag, dt)` —\ntilt-compensated magnetic heading corrects yaw at `kp_mag` and removes\ngyro-integration drift.) See [docs/control.md](control.md).\n\n### Altitude & climb rate (baro + ToF + accel → meters, m/s)\n\n```c\n#include \"fattos/fattos_fusion.h\"\n\nstatic FattosFusion fusion;\nfattos_fusion_init(&fusion);                       /* neutral gains     */\nfattos_fusion_ground(&fusion, ground_pressure_pa); /* lock AGL origin\n                                                      at boot           */\n\nfor (each control cycle) {\n    FattosFusionInput in = {0};\n    FattosSensorSample s;\n    if (fattos_sensor_read(\"baro\", &s) == FATTOS_OK) {\n        in.have_baro = true; in.pressure_pa = s.pressure_pa;\n    }\n    if (fattos_sensor_read(\"tof\", &s) == FATTOS_OK) {\n        in.have_tof = true; in.range_m = s.range_m;\n        in.cos_tilt = cos_tilt_from_attitude;      /* 1.0 if unknown */\n    }\n    if (fattos_sensor_read(\"imu\", &s) == FATTOS_OK) {\n        in.have_accel = true;\n        in.accel_z = world_frame_az_ms2;           /* gravity removed */\n    }\n    in.dt_s = dt_seconds;\n    float alt = fattos_fusion_step(&fusion, &in);\n    float climb = fusion.climb_rate_mps;\n}\n```\n\nThe estimator is a two-state complementary observer (no matrix math,\nbounded work per step):\n\n- **ToF** is trusted most *near the ground* (< 3 m, trust 0.7–0.9), zero\n  trust beyond its 3 m physical envelope.\n- **Barometric altitude** (ISA conversion `44330·(1−(p/p₀)^0.190263)` with\n  `p₀` the boot ground pressure) always contributes at trust 0.5 — it\n  works at any altitude, drifts slowly.\n- **Vertical acceleration** integrates the climb rate between updates.\n- Missing sensors degrade gracefully: no baro → ToF + accel; no ToF →\n  baro + accel; only baro → baro only. Absent fields are simply ignored.\n- The first near-ground ToF reading auto-learns the mount offset\n  (`tof_bias_m`); override with `fattos_fusion_set_tof_bias`.\n- Guards: `|dt|` clamped to 0.5 s, altitude clamped to sane physical\n  bounds, rate clamped ±100 m/s, non-finite inputs ignored.\n\nDiagnostics for a ground station: `fusion.baro_altitude_m`,\n`fusion.tof_height_m`, `baro_updates`, `tof_updates`, `accel_updates`.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Arming gate\n\n```c\nbool fatostrue(void);   /* exact name: fattos_sensors_prearm_ok() */\n```\n\n`fattos_sensors_prearm_ok()` returns true only when **every sensor in the\nmanifest** is `OK` (present, fresh, plausible). An empty manifest returns\nfalse (nothing to trust).\n\nThe vehicle state manager consults this **automatically**: if a manifest\nexists (`fattos_sensor_count() > 0`), its health is ANDed into the arming\ncondition and into the ARMED→FAILSAFE drop check — no wiring required:\n\n```c\nfattos_vehicle_update(&veh);\n/* ARMED requires: RC ok + arm switch + prearm_ok + sensors healthy\n   + supervisor healthy, held for arm_hold_ms */\n```\n\nConsequences worth knowing:\n\n- A chip that dies mid-flight drops the vehicle to FAILSAFE through the\n  same gate (via `fattos_sensors_prearm_ok()` evaluated each cycle).\n- A board with **no** manifest keeps the old semantics: only the app's\n  `fattos_vehicle_set_prearm()` bit gates arming.\n- Boot order matters: call `fattos_sensor_setup` before expecting the\n  gate to pass; a manifest that was never set up counts all-MISSING\n  (nothing arms until it is configured — the safe default).\n\n---\n\n## 11. Diagnostics & telemetry\n\n```c\nsize_t n = fattos_sensor_count();\nfor (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n    const char *name; uint8_t kind, health; uint32_t errors;\n    fattos_sensor_get_info(i, &name, &kind, &health, &errors);\n}\n```\n\nNames for logs: `fattos_sensor_kind_name(kind)` (\"MPU6050\", …),\n`fattos_sensor_health_name(health)` (\"OK\", \"MISSING\", \"ERROR\", \"STALE\",\n\"BOGUS\").\n\nGround-station transport is one call:\n\n```c\nuint8_t frame[128];\nsize_t len = fattos_telemetry_encode_status(seq, frame, sizeof(frame));\n/* frame payload: [0] sensor count, then 4 bytes per sensor\n   (kind, health, errors u16 LE), last byte = quarantined-task count */\n```\n\nSee [docs/communications.md](communications.md) for the framing layer.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Testing on the host simulator\n\n`platform/host/fattos_sim_bus.{h,c}` provides an emulated I2C bus carrying\nregister-faithful MPU6050, QMC5883, BMP280 and VL53L0X chips. The **exact\ndriver code** the MCU runs executes on the host — only the bus vtable is\nswapped.\n\n```c\n#include \"fattos_sim_bus.h\"\n\nfattos_sim_reset();\nfattos_sim_install(FATTOS_SENSOR_MPU6050, 0x68);\nfattos_sim_install(FATTOS_SENSOR_BMP280,  0x76);\n\nfloat accel[3] = {0, 0, 1.0f}, gyro[3] = {10, -5, 1}; /* g, dps */\nfattos_sim_imu_values(accel, gyro);\nfattos_sim_baro_values(101325.0f, 25.0f);\nfattos_sim_tof_value(0.42f);\n\nconst FattosBus *buses = fattos_sim_bus();\nfattos_sensor_setup(manifest, 4, buses, 1);\n```\n\n- Setpoints are *physical* (g, µT, Pa, m): the emulator converts them to\n  raw counts using the full-scale ranges **the driver actually\n  programmed**, so a wrong configuration register shows up as wrong data.\n- The BMP280 emulator solves for the raw ADC counts that produce the\n  requested pressure/temperature under the same shared compensation the\n  driver uses — round-trip exact to counting resolution.\n- `fattos_sim_set_present(kind, false)` unplugs a chip mid-test (address\n  NACKs) to exercise MISSING/ERROR health paths and prearm refusals.\n\nBuild wiring for a host test:\n\n```cmake\nadd_executable(my_tests tests/my_tests.c platform/host/fattos_sim_bus.c)\ntarget_link_libraries(my_tests PRIVATE fattos)\ntarget_include_directories(my_tests PRIVATE platform/host robot)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 13. Adding a new chip\n\nThe registry is designed so a new part touches *no existing call sites*:\n\n1. Add `FATTOS_SENSOR_<CHIP>` to `FattosSensorKind` (before `COUNT`).\n2. Write three static functions in `robot/fattos_sensor.c`:\n   `chip_probe(slot, &who)`, `chip_configure(slot)`,\n   `chip_read(slot, &sample)` — see\n   [docs/writing-drivers.md](writing-drivers.md) for full skeletons.\n3. Add the three pointers to `g_probe_tab[]`, `g_configure_tab[]`, and the\n   `driver_read` switch — one row each, same index.\n4. Add a `who_is_valid` case and a `fattos_sensor_kind_name` case.\n5. Optionally: emulate it in `fattos_sim_bus.c` to keep host tests.\n\nEverything else — manifest parsing, pacing, health, plausibility,\ncalibration, prearm, telemetry — is generic and starts working for the\nnew chip automatically.",
  "overview.md": "# FattOS — the robotics & autonomy OS for MCUs\n\nFattOS is a freestanding-friendly C11 OS foundation focused specifically on\nrobotics and autonomous applications — unlike general-purpose RTOSes\n(FreeRTOS, Zephyr) it ships the layers a robot actually needs: a supervised\nscheduler, IPC and instrumentation, a native INT8 neural-net runtime in a\nstatic arena, and a complete autonomy stack (vehicle arming/failsafe state\nmachine, behavior trees, motion profiles, RC input, ground-station\ncommands, safety-gated actuators). No dynamic allocation anywhere; every\nbuffer is static or caller-provided.\n\n## Kernel\n- Fixed-capacity task table, cooperative fixed-priority scheduling, 32-bit\n  millisecond clock with rollover-safe due checks. No malloc, ever.\n- Any `FATTOS_TICK_HZ`: public durations are milliseconds and converted with\n  `fattos_ms_to_ticks()` (rounds up); no 1 kHz lock-in.\n- Fair dispatch: equal-priority due tasks rotate through the scan, so no\n  task is systematically executed last every cycle.\n- Bounded drain: `fattos_run_pending(budget, &n)` runs at most `budget` due\n  callbacks — a drain loop can never spin forever and freeze the caller.\n- Per-task execution profiling at tick resolution: `exec_last_ticks`,\n  `exec_max_ticks`, `missed_releases`, `overrun_count`, and\n  `jitter_max_ticks` (worst lateness between due time and dispatch — your\n  scheduling-latency WCET on target).\n- Overrun fault hook (`fattos_set_fault_hook`): called in task context right\n  after a callback runs longer than its period — latch an e-stop there and\n  a late task becomes a safe stop, not a surprise.\n- Message queues: copy-in/copy-out, fixed capacity, caller storage,\n  protected by the platform irq lock (usable as ISR-to-task channel).\n- Software timers: one-shot and auto-reload, driven by a service task;\n  missed periods are skipped instead of bursting.\n- 32-bit event flag groups (cooperative poll semantics: any/all matching).\n- Fixed-block memory pool: O(1) alloc/free, no fragmentation, free list\n  kept inside free blocks.\n- Deadman watchdog: per-item timeouts; `fattos_wdt_kick_hardware()` services\n  the hardware watchdog only while every item is healthy, so a stalled task\n  causes a reset on MCU ports.\n\n## Edge AI\n- Static, aligned tensor arena (16 KiB default). The `FattosAIIO` seam is\n  the single inference entry point: it routes to the native INT8 runtime\n  when a model is bound (with exact shape checking) and refuses honestly\n  with `FATTOS_ERR_NOT_IMPLEMENTED` when no backend exists.\n- **Native INT8 neural-net runtime** (`fattos_nn`): fully-connected layers\n  with int32 accumulation and per-layer requantization, plus ReLU — small\n  dense models (anomaly detection, gesture/class classifiers) execute\n  entirely in the arena with no external interpreter.\n- Toolchain-friendly quantization: `fattos_nn_make_scale()` converts a float\n  scale into the (multiplier, shift) requant pair; `fattos_nn_quantize_value()`\n  and `fattos_features_quantize()` produce int8 inputs.\n- Sensor feature pipeline (`fattos_features`): rolling multi-axis window,\n  mean/RMS extraction, flattened feature vector, int8 quantization.\n- For CNNs or large models, integrate TensorFlow Lite Micro / CMSIS-NN\n  behind the `FattosAIIO` seam instead — the built-in runtime is deliberately\n  tiny.\n\n## Robotics & autonomy\n- **Board manifest** (`fattos_board`): declare ONCE in your app what is\n  connected where — motors, encoder geometry, IMU, RC link, e-stop GPIO —\n  and the OS derives its own tables: safety channels, odometry, vehicle\n  gate, RC input. Actuator writes go by NAME through the supervisor\n  (`fattos_board_write_motor`). Your code never touches raw pins for I/O\n  the OS manages; `AGENT.md` is the AI-agent-facing version of this.\n- **Go-to-goal navigation** (`fattos_nav`): distance-proportional speed\n  with heading-error fade (turn before you charge), arrival radius and\n  goal counters — feeds the yaw PID's heading target.\n- **Vehicle state manager** (`fattos_vehicle`): flight-controller arming\n  semantics — DISARMED → ARMED only after the RC arm switch is held for a\n  configured time with everything healthy; RC loss or supervisor fault →\n  FAILSAFE; latched e-stop → ESTOP with explicit recovery. One question\n  answers everything: `fattos_vehicle_can_actuate()`.\n- **Behavior trees** (`fattos_bt`): static, allocation-free trees of\n  sequence/selector/inverter nodes with leaf actions and conditions over a\n  blackboard — autonomous behavior becomes a data structure, not control\n  flow scattered across callbacks.\n- **Motion profiles** (`fattos_motion`): accel-limited trapezoidal\n  point-to-point reference generator with mid-profile retargeting and\n  no-overshoot clamping; feed the velocity reference as feedforward.\n- **RC input** (`fattos_rc`): SBUS decoder (16 channels, failsafe/frame-lost\n  flags, signal-loss timeout) feeding the vehicle arming gate.\n- **Ground-station commands** (`fattos_command`): typed command registry\n  over the telemetry link with explicit ack/nack — unknown ids and bad\n  payloads get answers, never silence.\n- **Leveled ring logger** (`fattos_log`): non-blocking, allocation-free,\n  overflow counted; a full log buffer can never stall a control loop.\n- PID controller: derivative-on-measurement, first-order D low-pass,\n  conditional anti-windup, **feedforward** (`fattos_pid_step_ff`) and\n  **bumpless transfer** (`fattos_pid_seed`).\n- 6-axis IMU attitude fusion (Mahony-style, gyro-bias integral feedback)\n  with **magnetometer heading correction** (`fattos_imu_update_mag`) that\n  removes yaw drift, plus direct yaw observation correction for GPS\n  heading.\n- Differential-drive odometry (exact arc integration) plus the inverse\n  velocity mixer with peak clamping.\n- Actuator safety supervisor: per-channel heartbeats with timeouts,\n  latched e-stop, fail-safe zero-duty on any fault, explicit recovery,\n  and a per-channel **slew-rate limiter** so a corrupted command cannot\n  slam a motor from 0 to full in one cycle (faults bypass slew and go\n  straight to zero — safety beats smoothness).\n  `fattos_safety_command()` is the safety-first actuator path: it re-checks\n  state freshly on every write, clamps duty to [0,1000] and forces zero\n  duty with `FATTOS_ERR_FAULT` whenever the system is not healthy — route\n  every motor command through it, never the raw HAL.\n- CRC-16/CRC-32 and a framed telemetry protocol (SOF, type, seq, len,\n  CRC) with a resyncing decoder state machine for host links.\n- HAL contract: GPIO, PWM, IMU sample, timer, idle, **irq lock/unlock**,\n  **hardware watchdog kick**.\n\n## Important limitations\n- The scheduler is **cooperative**, not preemptive. A task that blocks or\n  runs too long delays every other task. Overruns are measured\n  (`overrun_count`, `jitter_max_ticks`) and escalate through the fault\n  hook, but they cannot be preempted away. Do not use this version for\n  safety-critical motor control without the supervision layers and an\n  independently tested hardware e-stop.\n- No peripheral drivers yet: board ports must supply CAN, SPI/I²C sensor,\n  and encoder drivers behind the HAL (see `docs/porting-stm32.md`).\n- The host simulator is for logic tests, not MCU timing validation.\n- Execution profiling resolves in whole ticks; sub-millisecond WCET needs\n  a cycle-counter port.\n- The built-in NN runtime handles small dense INT8 models only; it is not a\n  drop-in TensorFlow Lite Micro replacement.\n- IMU fusion needs `libm` trig (`sinf`/`cosf`/`atan2f`/`asinf`) — fine on\n  FPU-class MCUs with newlib, swap for approximations when freestanding.\n- IMU yaw is gyro-integrated (no magnetometer) and drifts; the demo shows\n  this honestly (fused vs true yaw). Fuse an external heading source when\n  absolute yaw matters.\n- Watchdog/safety timeouts are evaluated when the supervising tasks run; a\n  hard hang of the whole scheduler is only caught by the hardware watchdog.\n- STM32 startup, linker script, clock tree, and real peripheral drivers\n  must be implemented for a selected board before flashing.\n\n## Build and test (host)\nRequirements: CMake 3.16+, C11 compiler (GCC/Clang/MSVC).\n\n```sh\ncmake -S . -B build -DFATTOS_BUILD_TESTS=ON -DFATTOS_BUILD_DEMO=ON\ncmake --build build\nctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure          # add -C Debug with Visual Studio\n./build/fattos_demo                                  # .\\build\\Debug\\fattos_demo.exe on MSVC\n```\n\nFive test suites cover the scheduler (priority, fairness, bounded drain,\nprofiling, jitter, fault hook), kernel services (queue, timers, events,\npool, watchdog), edge AI (NN exactness, features, AI seam), robotics (PID\nfeedforward/seed, IMU fusion with magnetometer, odometry, safety gating,\nCRC, telemetry) and autonomy (behavior trees, motion profiles, vehicle\narming/failsafe/estop, SBUS RC, command registry, logger). The demo runs a\ncomplete simulated robot: SBUS arming, a behavior-tree-driven trapezoidal\nmove, yaw-hold PID retargeted mid-run by a ground-station command,\nmagnetometer-corrected fusion, odometry, safety heartbeats, gated actuator\ncommands, deadman watchdog, NN inferences from a live feature window,\nframed telemetry, and event logging — 2 s of deterministic simulated time,\nending in explicit PASS/FAIL acceptance checks.\n\n## Project layout\n- `include/fattos/` public API and configuration\n- `kernel/` clock, scheduler, queue, timers, events, pool, watchdog\n- `robot/` PID, IMU fusion, odometry, safety supervisor, CRC, telemetry,\n  vehicle state manager, behavior trees, motion profiles, SBUS RC,\n  ground-station commands, logger\n- `ai/` tensor arena, INT8 NN runtime, feature pipeline\n- `platform/host/` host tick simulation\n- `platform/common/` default safe HAL implementation\n- `app/` integrated robot example\n- `tests/` deterministic suites (no hardware needed)\n- `docs/` architecture, autonomy guide, edge-AI model format, STM32\n  porting checklist\n- `AGENT.md` — authoritative context file for AI coding agents\n\n## Porting to an MCU\n1. Select the exact MCU/board and toolchain.\n2. Implement `FattosPlatform` functions: timer at `FATTOS_TICK_HZ`, GPIO,\n   PWM, IMU sample, irq lock/unlock (save/restore PRIMASK), watchdog kick.\n3. Call `fattos_tick_isr()` from a hardware timer interrupt at 1 kHz.\n4. Ensure startup code initializes `.data`/`.bss`, stack, clocks, and the\n   interrupt controller before `fattos_init()`.\n5. Keep ISR work bounded. Do not call task functions from an ISR.\n6. Verify actual WCET and deadlines on target and compare with the\n   scheduler's `exec_max_ticks`/`missed_releases` counters.\n7. Implement `fattos_platform_watchdog_kick()` and drive it through\n   `fattos_wdt_kick_hardware()`; add an independent hardware motor-disable\n   path.\n8. Export your model to INT8 and see `docs/edge-ai.md`; only integrate\n   TensorFlow Lite Micro if the model does not fit the built-in runtime.\n\n## API contract\nAll public functions return a `FattosStatus` where failure is possible. Task\ncallbacks and timer callbacks must be short, non-blocking, and return\npromptly. The scheduler executes at most one callback at a time.\n\n## License\nMIT. See `LICENSE`.\n"
};